paragraphs.text:
One of his seed became the firstfruits, chosen out of all,
and offered Himself a pure sacrifice and reconciled all.
The son of Abraham reconciled all with all by His death,
and won a favour that bears no comparison with created beings.
By His death He made up the lack of the sons of His kind,
and for this He was magnified above all, and all worship Him.
He receives the worship of all from all, together with the Creator,
and this is the wonder: that all worship Him together with the Divine Being.
A wonder full of newness has taken place among earthly beings:
their nature has become an object of worship together with the Divine Being.
See, our nature has attained a high rank with its Maker;
come, let us hasten to give thanks to the One who exalted us.
See, One of us has become a victor, who conquered and gave victory;
let us ask Him to help us in the contest of sufferings.
See, we are redeemed by the true death of One of our kind;
let us not turn back again to die in sin after we have come to life.
See, we are bought with the pure blood that is from our humanity;
let us not be sold into the evil slavery from which we escaped.
See, the God of all willed it and accepted One from among us all;
let us not again go astray in fleshly desires.
See, we have received the new hope of the life of the dead;
let us [?] in our minds that incorruptible adornment.
See, a smooth road has been trodden to the height above;
let us stir the wings of flight by our conduct.
See, we are invited to the wedding feast of the divine King;
let us prepare garments worthy of the name of that banquet.
See, we are named sons of the inheritance of the Heir of all;
let us bind our love in love for the name of the Head of our inheritance.
See, He has ascended and reigns above principalities and powers;
let us rejoice and exult that our kind has been magnified above all.
See, our lowly nature is glorified together with the One who chose it;
let us lift up our hands in thanksgiving for ourselves, since it is glorified.
See, the words spoken about our forming have been fulfilled,
that we should become an image worshipped together with our Creator.
See, heaven and earth worship us in that Son of Man;
let us give thanks to the One who exalted us above all.
Let our heart believe and our mouth confess without division
that the hidden Will put on our nature and renewed us in it.

He looked on it with the eye of the Spirit, that justice might be done.
The transgression of the commandment through desire for the fruit was plain to Him;
the cause of his life was not hidden from His knowledge.
It was no new thought that drew Him to his salvation;
with His very Being was hidden the mystery that was revealed at the end.
In the hiddenness of His love He was preparing the salvation of our life;
for the end He kept the great wealth of the power of His [?].
So that, when lowliness of heart was made perfect, it might receive His wealth,
in the fullness of times He opened the treasury of His divinity.
And He gave riches, not from deposits examined by laws;
hidden was the mystery of the Son and the Spirit, together with our salvation.
And through our [?] all learned the Trinity,
by which the spiritual beings received renewal:
that the Godhead is three hypostases, one in nature;
in us He opened the treasury of His mercies before His creatures.
The rational and the dumb entered and were set in the garden of life;
us He honored first and last, above all that came to be.
He called us His image and made us a dwelling of His divinity;
at the first He honored us by the fashioning that His hands wrought.
And at the completion His love dwelt in us, and He gives rest to all;
He sent to us none of the spiritual beings of His service.
The Word that is from Him He sent, and called us to knowledge of Him;
in the manner of a word, the Offspring of the Father went forth from the Father.
For the word is hidden in the soul, and goes forth and abides without change;
so His will went forth from the height and came to the depth.
And He prepared a dwelling in a fleshly creature for the will of His love,
as the soul and the word that comes from it are hidden from onlookers.
Letters, ink and page show it to the eyes of the senses;
the Word of the Father, with His hiddenness, was hidden from all.
His will painted on the page of our body, and we see His glory,
as the pen paints the word on the page only by a name.
The word is hidden in the soul, and the mouth renders it in speech;
so by a borrowed name the Word too dwelt in the womb of flesh.
And on the lyre of the members of our body His will made music;
the word is hidden in the soul and made manifest through deeds.
It is wholly in the soul and wholly with all, yet it is not seen;
on high He is with His Father, hidden in the Hidden One, and revealed in His love.
He is wholly in His Father and wholly in all, and is not bounded;
and if there is anything in the things made, it is as from Him.
He is with Him, and outside Him without separation;
how much more fitting is it to confess this of the hidden nature of Being!
For He is ever with His Begetter and with all;
[?] the word, the Only One went forth from His Begetter.
And the will of His love dwelt in the womb for the salvation of our life;
not in His nature did He confine Himself in the womb of flesh.
That will of His compounded flesh and made it His dwelling;
His nature, which holds all bounds, did not come within a bound.
His will put on a bounded thing and shut up all in it;
He willed to abolish the decree of the sentence of death that lay upon us.
And His will went up through mortality to the abolition of death:
